From: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] map-agent: borrow the measurement, not the identity

The rssi fallback for MLO clients could never write anything, so the defect
it was added to fix was never fixed.

wifi_sta_periodic_run() picks the link this BSS is entitled to report, and
when that link is idle falls back to the strongest link that measured
something. It did so by repointing `e` at the other link's entry - and
update_sta_entry() looks its argument up by e->macaddr in THIS ap's stalist:

	list_for_each_entry(s, &ap->stalist, list)
		if (!memcmp(s->macaddr, e->macaddr, 6))

The borrowed link's address is not in this ap's stalist; it lives on the
affiliated AP that carries it. The loop found nothing, the function returned
without writing, and the fallback was a no-op in exactly the case it existed
for.

It was added 2026-08-03 because an iPhone with three links reported 0 on all
of them and the controller stored rcpi 255. Measured again 2026-09-07 on the
bedroom node, unchanged: two of three legs read rssi 0 while the reply
carried -43 dBm for the leg that was actually being heard. That is also
where sta.rcpi = 128 comes from - a number matching none of the legs.

So copy the entry that belongs to this BSS and take only the signal from the
strongest link. The station keeps its own address, the lookup succeeds, and
the borrowed value lands. `sta` is reused rather than adding another
wifi_sta to a stack that already holds a whole wifi_mlsta.

Verify: with an MLO client parked on one link, the idle legs must report the
carrying leg's rssi instead of 0, and the controller must store an rcpi that
corresponds to a measured link.

Signed-off-by: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
---
--- a/src/agent.c
+++ b/src/agent.c
@@ -1553,27 +1553,56 @@
 		 * anything.
 		 */
 		{
-			struct wifi_sta *e = &mlsta.sta[0];
+			struct wifi_sta *m = &mlsta.sta[0];
 			int li;
 
 			for (li = 0; li < mlsta.num_link; li++) {
 				if (!memcmp(mlsta.sta[li].macaddr, s->macaddr, 6)) {
-					e = &mlsta.sta[li];
+					m = &mlsta.sta[li];
 					break;
 				}
 			}
 
-			if (e->rssi_avg == 0) {
+			/* Borrow the measurement, never the identity.
+			 *
+			 * The fallback below used to repoint the entry at the
+			 * strongest link. update_sta_entry() then looked THAT
+			 * link up by ITS address in THIS ap's stalist - where it
+			 * cannot be, because it lives on the affiliated AP that
+			 * carries it. The lookup failed silently, nothing was
+			 * written, and the symptom this fallback was added for
+			 * (2026-08-03: three links all reporting 0) survived it
+			 * untouched. Measured again 2026-09-07 on the bedroom
+			 * node: two of three legs still read rssi 0, with -43 dBm
+			 * sitting unused in the reply.
+			 *
+			 * Reuse `sta`, which the non-MLO branch leaves untouched,
+			 * rather than putting another wifi_sta on a stack that
+			 * already carries a whole wifi_mlsta.
+			 */
+			sta = *m;
+
+			if (sta.rssi_avg == 0) {
+				struct wifi_sta *best = NULL;
+
 				for (li = 0; li < mlsta.num_link; li++) {
 					if (mlsta.sta[li].rssi_avg == 0)
 						continue;
-					if (e->rssi_avg == 0 ||
-					    mlsta.sta[li].rssi_avg > e->rssi_avg)
-						e = &mlsta.sta[li];
+					if (!best || mlsta.sta[li].rssi_avg >
+						     best->rssi_avg)
+						best = &mlsta.sta[li];
+				}
+
+				/* Signal only. The address stays this link's, so
+				 * the station is found and the value lands. */
+				if (best) {
+					sta.rssi_avg = best->rssi_avg;
+					memcpy(sta.rssi, best->rssi,
+					       sizeof(sta.rssi));
 				}
 			}
 
-			update_sta_entry(a, ap, e);
+			update_sta_entry(a, ap, &sta);
 		}
 	}
 #endif
